SAMPLE = (
    "What the attacker does not have: the key. A great deal of ciphertext, often days or months of intercepted "
    "traffic. A guess about the language, usually a correct one. A guess about the message format: headers, "
    "salutations, standard phrases. The algorithm itself, because Kerckhoffs. The craft is to find which statistic "
    "of the plaintext survived the encryption, and then measure it carefully enough to turn a guess into a "
    "certainty. Every technique today is a measurement. None of them is a search through the key space. "
    "Ranking fails on short texts because the order of adjacent letters is unstable: swapping T and A costs almost "
    "nothing in probability. A numeric score fails gracefully instead. It gives the best candidate even when no "
    "candidate is obviously right, and it is comparable between candidates. That is what lets the whole attack be "
    "automated. A Vigenere ciphertext is m interleaved Caesar ciphers, one per key position. If you know m, you are "
    "finished: split into m cosets, break each with the scoring function from section one, assemble the key. If you "
    "do not know m, single-letter frequencies tell you nothing, because the aggregate distribution has been "
    "flattened by the mixing of m different shifts. So the entire problem reduces to one number. A repeated "
    "plaintext fragment aligned with the same part of the key produces a repeated ciphertext fragment. For that "
    "alignment to happen, the distance between the two occurrences must be a multiple of the key length. "
    "Repetition is structure, and structure is what cryptanalysis eats. Some repeats are coincidence, not "
    "structure, and a single coincidental distance destroys the greatest common divisor. Use longer repeats where "
    "you have them. A repeated four or five letter sequence is far less likely to be coincidence than a trigram, "
    "so weight it more heavily. Kasiski gives candidates, not an answer. Confirm with the index of coincidence. "
    "It is a single number computed from the ciphertext alone. No key, no guess about the plaintext, no "
    "decryption. And it is invariant under any monoalphabetic substitution: relabelling the letters does not "
    "change how often two of them match. If m is correct, every coset is a monoalphabetic cipher of a natural "
    "language sample, so each one has an index near the value of the language. If m is wrong, the cosets are "
    "still mixtures, so the average stays near the random floor. Why it works: the score improves gradually as "
    "the key gets closer to correct. A cipher whose score only improved at the exact key would defeat this "
    "completely, and that is one way of describing what a modern cipher achieves."
)